The period from 1736 to 1772 covers the first thirty-six years of the [[First Republic of Sinchew]]. It begins with the [[Unified Transition Council]] handing over to an elected [[General Assembly of Sinchew|Assembly]] and ends with the fragmentation of the party system that had governed the republic since 1740.

It is the period in which the republic established itself as a trading state rather than a besieged one, in which its economy began to turn from the western coast towards the industrial east, and in which the two parties that contested its politics for a generation took shape.

Presidents served four-year terms with no limit on re-election, though most served one. The president was elected by the Assembly, and the first vote of the Assembly's second session in each term was the presidential vote, held on 8 January. The first Assembly election was held on 1 October 1735, the Assembly took office on 1 January 1736, and the first president was elected on 8 January 1736.

== First presidency (1736–1740) ==

The first president was Ông Liông-seng (王隆生), one of the principal commanders of the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] and a member of the [[Unified Transition Council]]. He was elected by a majority of the Assembly on 8 January 1736.

=== Return to trade ===

The republic's first object was to reopen the trade the war had closed. Zong had adopted a policy of maximum isolation against Sinchew: trade and ordinary dealings with the colony's former markets were prohibited, and the court attempted to secure the same policy from its allies, [[Mitoyagi]] and the [[Sultanate of Maros]].

The prohibition was effective against Zong and its colonies and ineffective against its allies. Ships from Mitoyagi continued to call at Sinchew for cash crops throughout the period. The republic approached [[Volisania]], and began by exporting grain to [[Viceroyalty of Balboa|Balboa]] and [[Viceroyalty of Tapuya|Tapuya]]. Trade in cash crops was opened with [[Mercia]], [[Sarta]] and the [[Jacano|Jacan]] country, whose demand had until then been supplied almost entirely by Volisania and Burawa, which held what amounted to a monopoly in cotton and tobacco.

The reversal fell on Louyang. Zong had lost its principal source of cotton with the secession of Sinchew, and turned to the [[Louyang Colony]] to make up the yield, pressing a state with a far less plantation-based economy towards plantation agriculture.

The measures commanded general assent, and the republic's political class was agreed that international recognition came first; the disputes that divided it later had not yet formed.

Ông served two terms. He resigned midway through the second, in 1740, stating that he did not wish to hold the office indefinitely, and died a few months afterwards.

== Formation of parties (1740–1748) ==

Tân Ho̍k-chong was elected in 1740 and served until 1748. His administration was more divided than its predecessor's, and the domestic question that divided it was the representation of the western provinces.

Bills passed in these years favoured the western economy and the [[Tianism|Tianist]] interest: plantation agriculture, fishing, and the processing industries attached to them. The eastern provinces, and those around the former [[Hachinawa]] heartland in particular, had meanwhile invested in manufacturing, diversifying away from the plantation economy. The use of coal for processing, mined in the far east, began to move the country's economic centre away from [[Tamsui]] and the coast.

Two parties formed during the period, and they were regional in origin and remained so:

